[31.358673, "o", "/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info &&…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 19 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Now let me verify it works:\r\n[approval] bash: pip3 --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip3 --version 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 20 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -m pip --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3 -m pip --version 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 21 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Let me do a quick test install to confirm it can actually install package…\r\n[approval] bash: pip3 install --dry-run requests 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip3 install --dry-run requests 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 22 tool call(s) so far\r\nEverything is working now.\r\n\r\n**What was wrong:** The `pip` Python package files had been deleted from `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/`, but the metadata directory (`pip-24.3.1.dist-info`) was left behind. This caused `import pip` to fail with `ModuleNotFoundError` because pip's e"]
[31.358678, "o", "ntry point script tried to import the module but the actual `pip/` directory was gone.\r\n\r\n**What fixed it:** I removed the stale `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` metadata directory and re-ran `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade`, which extracted the bundled `pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl` from `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/` and restored the package.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=implementor model=qwen3.6-35b tools=22 surface=a5d46c1799a3\r\n"]
